{"cuisine/togolese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/togolese-cuisine","n":"Togolese cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Togo","intro":[["Togolese cuisine is the cuisine of the Togolese Republic, a country in West Africa. Staple foods in Togolese cuisine include maize, rice, millet, cassava, yam, plantain and beans. Maize is the most commonly consumed food in the Togolese Republic. Fish is a significant source of protein. People in Togo tend to eat at home, but there are also restaurants and food stalls."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"togolese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Togolese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Ndudu by Fafa (Togolese posts)","ref":"site/ndudu-by-fafa-blogspot-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ndudu-by-fafa.blogspot.com","note":"A handful of Togolese dishes from a London based cook with Ghanaian and Togolese family.","url":"https://ndudu-by-fafa.blogspot.com/search/label/Togolese%20Food","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/newari-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/newari-cuisine","n":"Newari cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Nepalese cuisine","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Nepal","intro":[["Newar cuisine, also referred to as Newari cuisine, is a subset of Nepalese cuisine that has developed over centuries among the Newars of the Nepal Mandala region in Nepal. Newa cuisine is more elaborate than most Nepalese cuisines because the Kathmandu Valley has exceptionally fertile alluvial soil and enough wealthy households to make growing produce more profitable than cultivating rice and other staples."],["Food is an integral part of the Newar culture. Dishes served during feasts and festivals have symbolic significance."],["Newa cuisine is recognized as one of the oldest food cultures in South Asia. Daily meals in Newa cuisine are commonly referred to as \"Jā Neu/जा नेउ\" or \"Newa Thali\". These meals typically consist of Taichin rice, lentil soup, a variety of green vegetables, assorted vegetable dishes, and meat items, accompanied by pickles. Traditionally, he morning meal is known as \"Jyaana/ज्या:न,\" the afternoon meal is called \"Baji/ बजी,\" and the evening meal is referred to as \"Beli/बेली.\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"newari-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newari_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Nepalese cuisine","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Chaku (Newari cuisine)","dish/chaku-newari-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Nepalese cuisine","cuisine/nepalese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/caucasian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/caucasian-cuisine","n":"Caucasian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["This article provides a list of dishes originating from the Caucasus. Caucasian culinary tradition includes that of Armenians, Azerbaijanis, Georgians, and of all other ethnic groups in the Caucasus, such as Chechens, Circassians, Ossetians, and many others."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caucasian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Kaukasis: A Culinary Journey through Georgia, Azerbaijan & Beyond","ref":"cookbook/kaukasis-a-culinary-journey-through-georgia-azerbaijan-beyond","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Olia Hercules, 2017","note":"Recipes gathered across the Caucasus, mostly Georgia and Azerbaijan.","url":"https://www.simonandschuster.ca/books/Kaukasis/Olia-Hercules/9781681883038","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Within Caucasian cooking","count":6,"links":[["Azerbaijani cuisine","cuisine/azerbaijani-cuisine"],["Chechen cuisine","cuisine/chechen-cuisine"],["Circassian cuisine","cuisine/circassian-cuisine"],["Dagestani cuisine","cuisine/dagestani-cuisine"],["Georgian cuisine","cuisine/georgian-cuisine"],["Ossetian cuisine","cuisine/ossetian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/niuean-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/niuean-cuisine","n":"Niuean cuisine","c":"#55b8ad","crumb":[["Oceania","cuisines#oceania"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Niue","intro":[["Niue is an island in the Southern Pacific, mostly inhabited by Polynesians. The plantations are mostly filled with manioc, taro and breadfruit, but banana trees can be found. The wide range of exotic plants in Niue includes taros, pawpaw, coconuts, bananas, yams, cassavas and breadfruits: All are intensively used in the local cuisine."],["The most significant ingredient when discussing the Niue's recipes are the fish and the vegetables. The staple ingredient is fish. Fish is consumed roasted, grilled, raw, and in soups or stews. There is a wide spectrum of edible and enjoyable fish species: tuna (ahi), dolphinfish (mahi mahi), parrot fish (pakati), barracuda (ono), coconut crabs and crayfish. In less populated areas, people prefer to eat vegetable meals, like taro roots or manioc."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"niuean-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Niuean_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Eat Pacific: The Pacific Island Food Revolution Cookbook","ref":"cookbook/eat-pacific-the-pacific-island-food-revolution-cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Robert Oliver (ed.), 2024","note":"Pacific Island Food Revolution cookbook with a Niue section.","url":"https://masseypress.ac.nz/books/eat-pacific","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"Oceanic cuisine"},"cuisine/st-lawrence-island-yupik-foodways":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/st-lawrence-island-yupik-foodways","n":"St. Lawrence Island Yupik foodways","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, St. Lawrence Island, Alaska","names":"Sivuqaghmiit, St. Lawrence Island Yupik","intro":[["The people of Gambell and Savoonga, on St. Lawrence Island in the Bering Sea, share their language with Yupik communities across the strait in Chukotka. The Alaska Native Heritage Center groups them with the Iñupiaq as people of the north, and the sea feeds them: walrus, ",["Whale","ingredient/whale-meat"]," and seal, with greens and berries from the island in summer."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"st-lawrence-island-yupik-foodways"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Muktuk","dish/muktuk"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Whale","ingredient/whale-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"dish/baked-pork-chop-rice":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/baked-pork-chop-rice","n":"Baked pork chop rice","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hong Kong cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Baked pork chop rice is a Hong Kong-style Western dish.  It is commonly served in cha chaan teng (茶餐廳). The dish combines Eastern and Western culinary influences. It consists of a base of fried rice with egg, which is topped with tomato sauce and cheese. This is then baked until the cheese is melted and then served."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-baked-pork-chop-rice"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baked_pork_chop_rice"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hong Kong cuisine","cuisine/hong-kong-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Pork chop","ingredient/pork-chop"],["Pork chops","ingredient/pork-chops"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"]]},{"h":"More from Hong Kong cuisine","links":[["Cha chaan teng","dish/cha-chaan-teng"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Tim Ho Wan","dish/tim-ho-wan"]]}]},"ingredient/pork-chops":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pork-chops","n":"Pork chops","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A pork chop is a thick slice of meat cut from the domestic pig, particularly one adjacent to a rib and usually served with it. It may be cooked in many ways, the simplest being fried or grilled, plain or coated with flour and dipped in egg and breadcrumbs. Numerous combinations of vegetables, fruits, spices and herbs are cooked with pork chops in the cuisines of many countries, including Austria, Britain, China, France, Germany, Italy, Spain and the US."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pork-chops"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pork_chops"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Hong Kong cuisine","cuisine/hong-kong-cuisine",[["Baked pork chop rice","dish/baked-pork-chop-rice"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Pork chop","ingredient/pork-chop"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Tomato sauce","ingredient/tomato-sauce"]]}]},"ingredient/mochiko":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/mochiko","n":"Mochiko","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Rice flour (also rice powder) is a form of flour made from finely milled rice. It is distinct from rice starch, which is usually produced by steeping rice in lye. Rice flour is a common substitute for wheat flour. It is also used as a thickening agent in recipes that are refrigerated or frozen since it inhibits liquid separation."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-mochiko"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mochiko"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Hawaiian cuisine","cuisine/cuisine-of-hawaii",[["Butter mochi","dish/butter-mochi"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}]},"dish/changsha-stinky-tofu":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/changsha-stinky-tofu","n":"Changsha stinky tofu","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hunan cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Changsha stinky tofu or stinky dry food, known in Chinese as Changsha chou doufu, also translated as Changsha-style stinky tofu, is a traditional snack in Changsha, Hunan, which belongs to Hunan cuisine. It is one of the renowned stinky tofu in Southern China."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-changsha-stinky-tofu"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Changsha_stinky_tofu"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hunan cuisine","cuisine/hunan-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Hunan cuisine","links":[["West Lake Restaurant","dish/west-lake-restaurant"]]}]},"dish/kerak-telor":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/kerak-telor","n":"Kerak telor","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Kerak telor is a Betawi traditional spicy omelet dish from Indonesia. It is made from glutinous rice cooked with egg and served with serundeng (fried shredded coconut), fried shallots, and dried shrimp as toppings. Kerak telor is considered a snack and not a main dish. The dish has become popular at the annual Jakarta Fair."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-kerak-telor"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerak_telor"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Chili","ingredient/chili"],["Dried shrimp","ingredient/dried-shrimp"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Glutinous rice","ingredient/glutinous-rice"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Serundeng","ingredient/serundeng"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}]},"dish/nevzine-tatl-s":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/nevzine-tatl-s","n":"Nevzine tatlısı","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Turkish cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Nevzine dessert is a Turkish dessert made with tahini, pekmez and walnut. It is a regional specialty of Kayseri province. It is usually served on Ramadan and on holidays."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-nevzine-tatl-s"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nevzine_tatl%C4%B1s%C4%B1"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Turkish cuisine","cuisine/turkish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Pekmez","ingredient/pekmez"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Ozlem's Turkish Table","ref":"site/ozlemsturkishtable-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"ozlemsturkishtable.com","note":"Turkish-born cook and teacher Ozlem Warren's blog of home-style Turkish recipes.","url":"https://ozlemsturkishtable.com/tag/turkish-recipes/page/9","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Turkish cuisine","links":[["Adana kebabı","dish/adana-kebab"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bici bici","dish/bici-bici"],["Boortsog","dish/boortsog"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Çiğ köfte","dish/cig-kofte"],["Çökertme kebabı","dish/cokertme-kebab"],["Dede (restaurant)","dish/dede-restaurant"],["Doner kebab","dish/doner-kebab"],["Düğün çorbası","dish/dugun-corbas"],["Eggplant jam","dish/eggplant-jam"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["İskender kebap","dish/iskender-kebap"],["Kavurma","dish/kavurma"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Kuru fasulye","dish/kuru-fasulye"],["Kyo